据信犯罪分子利用第三方供应商的用户名和密码进入了Home Depot网络的边界。
Criminals were believed to have used a third-party vendor's username and password to enter the perimeter of Home Depot's network.

5154
08:03:44,238 --> 08:03:50,212
攻击者随后能够在他们在美国和加拿大的自助结账系统上部署定制的恶意软件。
The attackers were then able to deploy custom-built malware on its self-checkout systems in the US and Canada.

5155
08:03:50,702 --> 08:03:52,100
接下来看第三个。
Moving on to number three.

5156
08:03:52,462 --> 08:03:57,508
正如大家所知，PlayStation 游戏系统是 Sony 最受欢迎的产品之一。
As you might be aware, the PlayStation Gaming System is one of Sony's most popular products.

5157
08:03:58,094 --> 08:04:04,772
不幸的是，在 2011 年 4 月，Sony 高管发现 PlayStation Network 出现了异常活动。
Unfortunately, in April 2011, Sony executives witnessed abnormal activity on the PlayStation Network.

5158
08:04:05,248 --> 08:04:16,484
这导致大约 7700 万 PlayStation 用户账号遭到入侵，并导致 PlayStation 3 和 PlayStation
Portable 主机用户无法访问此服务。
This resulted in the compromise of approximately 77 million PlayStation users' accounts and prevented users of PlayStation 3 and
PlayStation Portable consoles from accessing this service.

5159
08:04:17,070 --> 08:04:20,868
这迫使 Sony 在 4 月 20 日关闭了 PlayStation Network。
This forced Sony to turn off the PlayStation Network on April 20th.

5160
08:04:21,262 --> 08:04:28,676
5 月 4 日，Sony 确认这 7700 万个账户中的个人身份信息均已泄露。
On May 4th, Sony confirmed that personally identifiable information from each of the 77 million accounts had been exposed.

5161
08:04:28,974 --> 08:04:31,008
故障持续了 23 天。
The outage lasted for 23 days.

5162
08:04:31,374 --> 08:04:35,076
Sony 几乎每天都会发布关于系统故障的公告。
Sony released almost daily announcements concerning the system outage.

5163
08:04:35,502 --> 08:04:47,972
最后，据信 Sony 投入了约 1.7 亿美元来改进网络安全、调查此次攻击，
并支付受影响消费者的补偿费用。
In the end, Sony is believed to have invested approximately $170 million to improve the network security, to investigate the attack,
and to cover the expenses of caring for the consumers that had been affected.

5164
08:04:48,430 --> 08:04:50,368
现在让我们看下一个攻击案例。
Now let's move on to the next attack.

5165
08:04:50,798 --> 08:04:56,196
在第二项中，2017 年 5 月发生了一次最危险的网络攻击之一。
In at number two, in May 2017, one of the most dangerous cyber attacks took place.

5166
08:04:56,686 --> 08:05:01,380
它被称为 WannaCry 勒索软件攻击，是由 WannaCry 加密蠕虫引起的。
It was known as the WannaCry ransomware attack, caused by the WannaCry crypto worm.

5167
08:05:01,648 --> 08:05:08,932
受害者是那些使用不支持版本的 Microsoft Windows 以及未安装最新安全更新的用户。
The victims were the users that used the unsupported version of
Microsoft Windows and those who hadn't installed the new security update.

5168
08:05:09,422 --> 08:05:15,044
这次攻击并非像其他攻击那样通过网络钓鱼，而是通过暴露的易受攻击的 SMB 端口进行的。
This did not take place through phishing like other attacks, but through an exposed vulnerable SMB port.

5169
08:05:15,488 --> 08:05:20,688
这次攻击起源于亚洲，随后在一天内传遍全球。
The attack originated in Asia and then eventually spread across the globe in a day.

5170
08:05:20,974 --> 08:05:25,028
在 150 个国家中，有超过 20 万台电脑被感染。
More than 200,000 computers were infected across 150 countries.

5171
08:05:25,422 --> 08:05:30,768
WannaCry 加密蠕虫锁定了用户无法进入目标系统，并加密了他们的数据。
The WannaCry crypto worm locked the users out of the targeted systems and encrypted their data.

5172
08:05:30,990 --> 08:05:38,244
用户被要求支付 300 到 600 美元的赎金，必须通过 Bitcoin 支付才能换回数据。
The users were asked for a ransom of $300 to $600, which had to be paid via Bitcoin in exchange for their data.

5173
08:05:38,862 --> 08:05:42,768
这次攻击对私人机构和政府机构都造成了巨大损失。
This attack took a toll on both private and government organizations.

5174
08:05:43,086 --> 08:05:46,820
它造成的损失从数亿美元到数十亿美元不等。
It resulted in damages from hundreds of millions to billions of dollars.

5175
08:05:47,662 --> 08:05:57,796
在短短几天内，Microsoft 发布的紧急补丁阻止了攻击，同
时发现紧急停机开关也防止了受感染的电脑传播加密蠕虫。
In a matter of a few days, the emergency patches released by Microsoft halted the attack,
also the discovery of a kill switch prevented the infected computers from spreading the crypto worm.

5176
08:05:58,288 --> 08:06:03,524
几个国家的安全专家认为，这次攻击背后是某国。
Security experts in a few countries believed that a certain country was behind this attack.

5177
08:06:04,046 --> 08:06:06,500
现在最后，让我们看看第一名是什么。
Now finally, let's see what we have at number one.

5178
08:06:07,022 --> 08:06:14,180
二十多年前，在 1999 年 3 月，Melissa 病毒，一种大规模邮件微型病毒被发布。
More than two decades ago, in March 1999, the Melissa virus, a mass mailing microvirus, was released.

5179
08:06:14,382 --> 08:06:19,268
它针对基于 Microsoft Word 和 Outlook 的系统，并产生了巨大的网络流量。
It targeted Microsoft Word and Outlook based systems and created considerable network traffic.

5180
08:06:19,950 --> 08:06:22,864
Melissa 病毒通过电子邮件感染电脑。
Melissa virus infected computers via emails.

5181
08:06:23,278 --> 08:06:27,012
这封邮件看起来像是一条重要消息，但其实是假的。
The email would look like an important message, but it was fake.

5182
08:06:27,470 --> 08:06:36,464
如果收件人在邮件中打开附件并下载文档，然后用
Microsoft Word 打开它，病毒就会释放到他们的电脑上。
If the recipient opens the attachments in the email and downloads the document,
then opens it with Microsoft Word, a virus was released onto their computers.

5183
08:06:36,974 --> 08:06:46,544
接着，它会向受害者联系人列表中的前 50 个人发送大规模邮件，
并禁用 Microsoft Word 和 Microsoft Outlook 上的多个安全功能。
This would then mass mail itself to the first 50 people in the victim's contact list
and then disable multiple safeguard features on Microsoft Word and Microsoft Outlook.

5184
08:06:47,184 --> 08:06:49,504
这开始像野火一样在互联网上蔓延。
This began spreading like a wildfire across the internet.

5185
08:06:50,254 --> 08:06:50,624
David L.
David L.

5186
08:06:50,784 --> 08:06:51,984
Smith 发布了那个病毒。
Smith released the virus.

5187
08:06:52,464 --> 08:06:55,744
这个病毒造成了近 8000 万美元的损失。
The virus caused nearly $80 million worth of damages.

5188
08:06:56,206 --> 08:06:57,904
它并没有窃取数据或金钱。
It did not steal data or money.

5189
08:06:58,064 --> 08:06:59,584
然而，它造成了巨大的混乱。
However, it caused havoc.

5190
08:07:00,014 --> 08:07:03,184
全球范围内几乎有 100 万个电子邮件账户受到干扰。
Almost one million email accounts were disrupted worldwide.

5191
08:07:03,424 --> 08:07:07,104
相关机构不堪重负，有些甚至不得不完全关闭。
Agencies were overloaded, and some had to be shut down entirely.

5192
08:07:07,264 --> 08:07:10,244
而且某些地点的网络流量变慢了。
And internet traffic in some locations was slowed down.

5193
08:07:10,624 --> 08:07:15,236
好了，到这里我们就结束了关于史上十大电脑黑客攻击的视频。
So with that, we've come to the end of this video on the top ten computer hacks of all time.

5194
08:07:15,662 --> 08:07:16,868
你同意我们的这份名单吗？
Do you agree with our list?

5195
08:07:17,390 --> 08:07:23,024
如果你知道历史上其他重大或有趣的电脑黑客攻击，请在下面的评论区告诉我们。
If you're aware of other great, interesting computer hacks in history, do let us know in the comment section below.

5196
08:07:23,264 --> 08:07:25,184
现在让我们来了解什么是密码学。
Now let's understand what is cryptography.

5197
08:07:25,504 --> 08:07:32,196
密码学就像一面隐形的盾牌，保护你的数据安全，确保只有正确的人才能读取它。
cryptography is an invisible shield that keeps your data safe, making sure only the right person can read it.

5198
08:07:32,544 --> 08:07:37,124
简单来说，密码学就像是把你的信息变成一段秘密代码。
You can simply put cryptography is like turning your message into a secret code.

5199
08:07:37,424 --> 08:07:40,356
如果别人看到了，它看起来就像是乱码。
If someone else sees it, it looks like nonsense.

5200
08:07:40,464 --> 08:07:43,504
但你的朋友可以轻松地对其进行解码并阅读。
But your friend can easily decode and read it.

5201
08:07:43,744 --> 08:07:49,540
我们每天都在使用密码学来保护信息、支付和个人信息免受黑客侵害。
We use cryptography every day to protect messages, payments, and personal information from hackers.

5202
08:07:49,744 --> 08:07:56,260
所以简而言之，它就是创建和破解秘密代码的艺术，旨在保护我们的数字世界安全。
So, in short, it's the art of creating and solving secret codes to keep our digital world secure.

5203
08:07:56,544 --> 08:08:05,584
但你有没有想过，你的私人信息是如何保持私密的，
或者银行如何在不见到你本人的情况下信任在线交易？
But have you ever wondered how your private messages stay private or how banks trust online transactions without seeing you in person?

5204
08:08:05,904 --> 08:08:07,076
让我们来找出答案。
Let's find that out.

5205
08:08:07,264 --> 08:08:10,724
现在让我们简要讨论一下秘密代码的历史。
So now let's discuss a brief history of secret codes.

5206
08:08:11,024 --> 08:08:14,660
为了更好地理解密码学，让我们回到过去。
To understand cryptography better, let's travel back in time.

5207
08:08:14,944 --> 08:08:19,664
几千年前，人们就已经在尝试保护秘密了。
Thousands of years ago, people were already trying to protect secrets.

5208
08:08:19,824 --> 08:08:24,784
最早的方法之一就是 Julius Caesar 使用的凯撒密码。
One of the earliest methods was the Caesar cipher used by Julius Caesar.

5209
08:08:24,944 --> 08:08:30,628
他只是简单地移动字母顺序，这样敌人就无法理解他的信息。
He simply shifted letters in the alphabet so that enemies couldn't understand his messages.

5210
08:08:31,024 --> 08:08:32,900
简单但对于那个时代来说非常聪明。
Simple but clever for its time.

5211
08:08:33,390 --> 08:08:41,984
快进到第二次世界大战，德国军队使用了一种名为 Enigma 的机器，它能生成极其复杂的代码。
Fast forward to World War II, the German army used a machine called Enigma, which created extremely complex codes.

5212
08:08:42,144 --> 08:08:48,452
破解 Enigma 帮助改变了世界，并标志着密码学历史上的一个重大时刻。
Breaking Enigma helped change the world and marked a huge moment in the history of cryptography.

5213
08:08:48,704 --> 08:08:58,852
从秘密信件和战争信息开始，密码学慢慢演变成了
我们现在所说的现代密码学，由数学和计算机驱动。
From secret letters and war messages, cryptography slowly evolved into
what we now call modern cryptography, powered by mathematics and computers.

5214
08:08:59,342 --> 08:09:02,916
现在，这么古老的东西至今仍在使用，难道不神奇吗？
Now, isn't it amazing that something so old still is today?

5215
08:09:03,104 --> 08:09:08,420
现在让我们在不涉及复杂技术概念的情况下，来了解加密到底是怎么工作的。
So now let's understand how does encryption actually works without any technical confusion.

5216
08:09:08,704 --> 08:09:11,344
你可以把加密想象成一把锁和一把钥匙。
So you can think of encryption like a lock and key.

5217
08:09:11,504 --> 08:09:14,628
你的信息就是那把锁，而钥匙就是用来打开它的工具。
Your message is a lock and the key is what opens it.

5218
08:09:14,944 --> 08:09:16,868
主要有两种类型的钥匙。
There are two main types of keys.

5219
08:09:17,184 --> 08:09:19,172
第一种是对称加密。
First one is symmetric encryption.

5220
08:09:19,504 --> 08:09:22,340
使用相同的钥匙来解锁信息。
The same key is used to unlock the messages.

5221
08:09:22,574 --> 08:09:26,468
这种方式很快，但双方必须以某种安全的方式共享这把钥匙。
This is fast, but both people must somehow share the key safely.

5222
08:09:26,864 --> 08:09:29,264
第二种是非对称加密。
Second one is asymmetric encryption.

5223
08:09:29,344 --> 08:09:32,324
现在有两把钥匙：公钥和私钥。
Now there are two keys, public key and private key.

5224
08:09:32,624 --> 08:09:36,964
公钥用来锁上信息，但只有私钥才能把它打开。
The public key locks the message, but only the private key can unlock it.

5225
08:09:37,184 --> 08:09:38,500
现在有个问题。
Now here's a question.

5226
08:09:38,784 --> 08:09:42,884
如果每个人都能看到公钥，为什么他们就没法读取信息呢？
If everyone can see the public key, why can't they read the message?

5227
08:09:43,264 --> 08:09:48,036
因为只有私钥能打开它，而且那把钥匙是保密的。
Because only the private key can open it and that key is kept secret.

5228
08:09:48,304 --> 08:09:49,824
所以你看，其实就是这么简单。
So you see it's that simple.

5229
08:09:50,064 --> 08:09:54,276
现在让我们来看看一些你可能听过的一些著名的密码学算法。
Now let's meet some famous cryptography algorithms that you may heard of.

5230
08:09:54,510 --> 08:09:55,744
我们来聊聊 RSA。
Let's talk about RSA.

5231
08:09:56,110 --> 08:10:00,704
RSA 是互联网上最重要的一种加密方法之一。
Now, RSA is one of the most important encryption methods used in the internet.

5232
08:10:01,104 --> 08:10:05,572
它的工作原理是利用非常大的质数来创建加密密钥。
It works by using very large prime numbers to create encryption keys.

5233
08:10:05,904 --> 08:10:13,220
这些数字对于计算机来说很容易相乘，但一旦合并，就极难被拆解开来。
Now these numbers are easy for a computer to multiply together but extremely hard to break apart once they are combined.

5234
08:10:13,584 --> 08:10:15,812
这就是 RSA 安全的原因。
This is what makes RSA secure.

5235
08:10:16,224 --> 08:10:19,744
RSA 使用两把钥匙，一把公钥和一把私钥。
RSA uses two keys, a public key and a private key.

5236
08:10:19,904 --> 08:10:24,704
公钥与所有人共享，用于锁定或加密数据。
The public key is shared with everyone and it is used to lock or encrypt the data.

5237
08:10:24,864 --> 08:10:30,544
私钥则保密，它是唯一能解锁或解密数据的钥匙。
The private key is kept secret and it is the only key that can unlock or decrypt the data.

5238
08:10:30,704 --> 08:10:37,764
所以即使有人在网络传输过程中截获了加密信息，如果没有私钥，他们也无法读取。
So even if someone intercepts the encrypted message while it's traveling online, they cannot read it without the private key.

5239
08:10:37,984 --> 08:10:46,864
简单来说，RSA 确保即使在互联网这种开放或公共网络中，也只有预定的人能读取信息。
So, in simple terms, RSA makes sure that only the intended person can read the message,
even in an open or public network like the internet.

5240
08:10:47,438 --> 08:10:51,716
现在，是时候聊聊我们名单上的第二个算法了，也就是 AES。
Now, it's time to talk about our second algorithm on our list, which is AES.

5241
08:10:52,016 --> 08:10:55,076
这个名字代表高级加密标准。
This one stands for Advanced Encryption Standard.

5242
08:10:55,376 --> 08:11:00,420
现在，AES 是当今世界上应用最广泛的加密方法之一。
Now, AES is one of the most widely used encryption methods in the world today.

5243
08:11:00,846 --> 08:11:08,336
它被用于智能手机、笔记本电脑、云存储、银行系统，
甚至像 Netflix 或 Spotify 这样的流媒体平台。
It is used in smartphones, laptops, cloud storage, banking system, and even streaming platforms like Netflix or Spotify.

5244
08:11:08,576 --> 08:11:14,896
AES 是对称加密算法，这意味着使用相同的钥匙来锁定和解锁数据。
AES is symmetric encryption algorithm, which means the same key is used to lock and unlock the data.

5245
08:11:15,056 --> 08:11:19,296
这让它运行速度非常快，尤其是在处理大量信息时。
Now this makes it very fast, especially when handling large amounts of information.

5246
08:11:19,456 --> 08:11:26,116
那么，AES 的特别之处在于它不仅速度快，而且极其强大且值得信赖。
Now, what makes AES special is that it not only is fast but also extremely strong and trusted.

5247
08:11:26,416 --> 08:11:33,376
全球的政府、公司和安全专家都依靠 AES 来保护敏感数据。
Governments, companies, and security experts around the world rely on AES to protect sensitive data.

5248
08:11:33,646 --> 08:11:38,096
所以，简单来说，AES 是现代加密的支柱。
So, in simple terms, AES is the workhorse of modern encryption.

5249
08:11:38,256 --> 08:11:42,692
每当你使用数字设备时，它都在默默保护你的数据。
It quietly protects your data every time you use a digital device.

5250
08:11:43,086 --> 08:11:46,628
接下来是像 SHA 256 这样的哈希函数。
Then there are hash functions like SHA 256.

5251
08:11:46,896 --> 08:11:49,444
现在，哈希函数与加密是不同的。
Now, hash functions are different from encryption.

5252
08:11:49,616 --> 08:11:51,456
它们并不用于锁定或解锁数据。
They do not lock or unlock data.

5253
08:11:51,616 --> 08:12:00,196
相反，它们接收任何输入，比如密码、文件或消息，
并将其转换为固定长度的字符字符串，称为哈希。
Instead, they take any input, a password, a file, or a message, and it turns into a fixed length string of characters called a hash.

5254
08:12:00,416 --> 08:12:02,756
你可以把它想象成数字指纹。
You can think of it as a digital fingerprint.

5255
08:12:03,056 --> 08:12:12,656
那么，SHA 256 强大的地方在于，即使原始数据发生微小
的变化，比如改动一个字母，也会生成完全不同的哈希。
Now, what makes SHA 256 powerful is that even a tiny change in the original
data, like changing one letter, creates a completely different hash.

5256
08:12:12,846 --> 08:12:17,700
而且一旦生成了哈希，就无法通过它还原回原始数据。
And once a hash is created, it cannot be reversed to get the original data back.

5257
08:12:18,016 --> 08:12:24,096
很多人认为 SHA 256 实际上是用于 Bitcoin 的，但它其实到处都在使用。
Many people think that SHA 256 is actually used for Bitcoin, but it's used everywhere.

5258
08:12:24,336 --> 08:12:33,796
网站用它来安全地存储密码，系统用它来验证数据完整性，确保数据没有被更改或被篡改。
Websites use it to store passwords safely and systems use it to verify data integrity,
making sure that data has not been changed or tampered with.

5259
08:12:34,016 --> 08:12:42,308
所以，简单来说，SHA 256 帮助确认数据是真实、未变且可信的，而无需泄露数据本身。
So, in simple words, SHA 256 helps confirm that data is real, unchanged, and trustworthy without revealing the data itself.

5260
08:12:42,736 --> 08:12:47,456
以上就是一些你应该了解的著名密码学算法。
So these were some famous algorithms for cryptography you should look up to.

5261
08:12:47,696 --> 08:12:51,332
现在让我们看看如何在现实生活中使用密码学。
Now let's look at how you can use cryptography in real life.

5262
08:12:51,696 --> 08:12:56,644
你每天都在与密码学互动，而且往往甚至意识不到。
You can interact with cryptography every single day, often without even realizing it.

5263
08:12:56,816 --> 08:13:02,884
现在，每当你看到浏览器中的 HTTPS 时，你的连接就是通过加密受到保护的。
Now, whenever you see HTTPS in your browser, your connection is protected using encryption.

5264
08:13:03,216 --> 08:13:12,256
这意味着你发送的任何数据，比如密码、消息或支付详情，都被锁住了，
这样黑客在数据传输到互联网的过程中就无法读取。
That means any data you send, like passwords, messages, or payment details,
is locked so hackers can't read it while it travels across the internet.

5265
08:13:12,750 --> 08:13:15,856
像 WhatsApp 这样的应用使用端到端加密。
Apps like WhatsApp use end-to-end encryption.

5266
08:13:16,016 --> 08:13:21,696
你的消息在你的手机上被加密，只能在接收者的设备上被解密。
Your messages are encrypted on your phone and can only be decrypted on receiver's device.

5267
08:13:21,856 --> 08:13:27,716
甚至连 WhatsApp 本身也无法读取你的对话，从而保持你的聊天私密且安全。
Not even WhatsApp itself can read your conversations, keeping your chats private and secure.

5268
08:13:28,016 --> 08:13:32,256
密码学也是加密货币和区块链技术的基石。
Cryptography is also the backbone of cryptocurrency and blockchain technology.

5269
08:13:32,462 --> 08:13:41,444
现在，区块链不再依赖银行或中间人，而是利用密码学技术和数学来验证交易并建立信任。
Now instead of relying on banks or middlemen, blockchain uses cryptographic
techniques and mathematics to verify transactions and build trust.

5270
08:13:41,696 --> 08:13:50,176
所以，简单来说，密码学在后台默默运行，每天都在保护你的数据、你的隐私和你的数字生活。
So, in simple words, cryptography works silently in background,
protecting your data, your privacy, and your digital life every single day.

5271
08:13:50,336 --> 08:13:59,556
既然你已经看到了密码学是如何深度融入你的日常生活，现在让我们更进一步，
了解密码学家实际上是如何思考的。
So now that you have seen how deeply cryptography is woven into your everyday life,
let's take a step further and understand how cryptographers actually think.

5272
08:13:59,856 --> 08:14:02,896
好消息是，你不需要成为数学天才。
The good news is you don't need to be a math genius.

5273
08:14:03,056 --> 08:14:06,084
真正重要的是好奇心和逻辑思维。
What really matters is curiosity and logical thinking.

5274
08:14:06,256 --> 08:14:16,356
密码学建立在数论和模运算等简单概念之上。你可以
把模运算想象成时钟，过了 12 点后，它会回到 1。
Cryptographies build on simple ideas like number theory and modular math.
You can think of modular math like a clock after 12, it goes back to 1.

5275
08:14:16,656 --> 08:14:22,468
这个简单的概念足够强大，可以创建极难破解的安全系统。
This simple concept is powerful enough to create security systems that are extremely hard to break.

5276
08:14:22,736 --> 08:14:24,496
接下来是质数。
Then we've got prime numbers.

5277
08:14:24,656 --> 08:14:26,976
质数也起到了巨大的作用。
The prime numbers also play a huge role.

5278
08:14:27,136 --> 08:14:32,736
它们很容易相乘，但极难反推回原始数值。
They are easy to multiply together, but incredibly difficult to break back into their original values.

5279
08:14:32,896 --> 08:14:37,636
正是这种单向的难度，保证了许多加密系统的安全性。
Now, this one-way difficulty is what keeps many encryption systems secure.

5280
08:14:37,936 --> 08:14:42,436
但这里有一个重要的事实：没有任何系统是百分之百完美的。
But here's an important truth: no system is ever 100% perfect.

5281
08:14:42,816 --> 08:14:52,176
攻击者并不总是破解数学难题，相反，他们利用弱
密码、泄露的密钥、糟糕的系统设计或过时的算法。
Attackers don't just always break the math, instead, they exploit weak
passwords, leaked keys, poor system design, or outdated algorithms.

5282
08:14:52,336 --> 08:15:00,708
这就是为什么密码学家总是先尝试像攻击者一样思考，以便在别人发现之前找出并修复漏洞。
Now that's why cryptographers always try to think like attackers first to find out and fix weaknesses before someone else's does.

5283
08:15:01,136 --> 08:15:07,652
